The same state can be reached through different paths, so I stored the answer for each index. `dp[index]` means the minimum energy needed to reach the last stone from that index.

The helper checks all jumps from `1` to `k`. A jump is only limited by the remaining number of stones, which also handles the last few positions safely.

```java
class Solution {
    public int frogJump(int[] heights, int k) {
        int[] dp = new int[heights.length];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, -1);
        return solve(0, heights, k, dp);
    }

    private int solve(int index, int[] heights, int k, int[] dp) {
        if (index == heights.length - 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (dp[index] != -1) {
            return dp[index];
        }

        int best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int lastJump = Math.min(k, heights.length - 1 - index);

        for (int jump = 1; jump <= lastJump; jump++) {
            int nextIndex = index + jump;
            int cost = Math.abs(heights[index] - heights[nextIndex]);
            int candidate = cost + solve(nextIndex, heights, k, dp);
            best = Math.min(best, candidate);
        }

        dp[index] = best;
        return best;
    }
}
```

A small edge case is `k = 1`. In that case, every position has only one possible next stone, so the code simply adds the cost of consecutive jumps.

Time complexity: `O(n * k)`, since each index is solved once and checks at most `k` jumps.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the memo array and recursion stack.

Only the previous `k` DP values are needed to calculate the next value. This version keeps those values in a circular array instead of storing the full table.

The circular position is `index % (k + 1)`. Before writing a new value, the old value in that slot is replaced, because it is now more than `k` positions behind and will never be used again.

```java
class Solution {
    public int frogJump(int[] heights, int k) {
        int n = heights.length;
        int windowSize = k + 1;
        int[] recent = new int[windowSize];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(recent, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        recent[0] = 0;

        for (int index = 1; index < n; index++) {
            int best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
            int firstPrevious = Math.max(0, index - k);

            for (int previous = firstPrevious; previous < index; previous++) {
                int previousCost = recent[previous % windowSize];
                int jumpCost = Math.abs(heights[index] - heights[previous]);
                best = Math.min(best, previousCost + jumpCost);
            }

            recent[index % windowSize] = best;
        }

        return recent[(n - 1) % windowSize];
    }
}
```

The important limit is `k >= 1`, so the first stone's value remains available while computing the next one. This saves memory, although the full array version is a little easier to read.

Time complexity: `O(n * k)`.
Space complexity: `O(k)`, excluding the input array.

I kept the first version as a direct recursive solution. From stone `i`, the frog can choose any next stone from `i + 1` through `i + k`. The cost of a jump is the height difference, so each branch adds `abs(heights[i] - heights[next])`.

This is easy to follow, but it repeats the same suffix many times. It is mainly useful for understanding the recurrence, not for large inputs.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int frogJump(vector<int>& heights, int k) {
        return solve(0, heights, k);
    }

private:
    int solve(int index, vector<int>& heights, int k) {
        if (index == static_cast<int>(heights.size()) - 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        int best = INT_MAX;
        int lastJump = min(k, static_cast<int>(heights.size()) - 1 - index);

        for (int jump = 1; jump <= lastJump; ++jump) {
            int next = index + jump;
            int jumpCost = abs(heights[index] - heights[next]);
            int remainingCost = solve(next, heights, k);

            if (remainingCost != INT_MAX) {
                best = min(best, jumpCost + remainingCost);
            }
        }

        return best;
    }
};
```

For example, with `heights = [10, 20, 10]` and `k = 2`, the frog compares the path `0 -> 1 -> 2` with the direct path `0 -> 2`. The direct jump costs `0`, so that is selected.

Time complexity: `O(k^(n-1))` in the worst case, where `n` is the number of stones.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack.

This treats every stone as a graph node. From index `i`, there is an edge to each of the next `k` indices, and the edge weight is the absolute height difference. Dijkstra's algorithm then finds the cheapest route to the final stone.

It is more code than the direct DP, but it is a different way to view the problem and works because all jump costs are non-negative.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int frogJump(vector<int>& heights, int k) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(heights.size());
        vector<int> distance(n, INT_MAX);
        priority_queue<pair<int, int>,
                       vector<pair<int, int>>,
                       greater<pair<int, int>>> minHeap;

        distance[0] = 0;
        minHeap.push({0, 0});

        while (!minHeap.empty()) {
            auto [currentCost, index] = minHeap.top();
            minHeap.pop();

            if (currentCost != distance[index]) {
                continue;
            }

            if (index == n - 1) {
                return currentCost;
            }

            int lastNext = min(n - 1, index + k);
            for (int next = index + 1; next <= lastNext; ++next) {
                int jumpCost = abs(heights[index] - heights[next]);
                int newCost = currentCost + jumpCost;

                if (newCost < distance[next]) {
                    distance[next] = newCost;
                    minHeap.push({newCost, next});
                }
            }
        }

        return distance[n - 1];
    }
};
```

The priority queue can contain old entries, so the `currentCost != distance[index]` check skips them. With valid input, the last stone is always reachable because a jump of one is allowed.

Time complexity: `O(n * k * log n)` because there can be up to `n * k` edges.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for distances and the priority queue.

