## Intuition

This is the **Frog Jump with K distances** problem, and it has **optimal substructure**. The minimum energy needed to reach stair `i` depends only on the minimum energy needed to reach one of the previous `k` stairs (`i-1, i-2, ..., i-k`), plus the cost of the final jump from that stair to `i`.

If `dp[i]` is the minimum energy to reach index `i`:

`dp[i] = min over j in [1..k] of ( dp[i-j] + |heights[i] - heights[i-j]| )`

Since `dp[i]` only depends on smaller indices, we can fill the array from left to right (**bottom-up DP**). This removes recursion entirely: no stack overhead and no risk of stack overflow.

## Approach

1. **Define the state:** `dp[i]` stores the minimum energy required to reach index `i` starting from index `0`.

2. **Initialisation:** Fill `dp` with `1e9` (acting as "infinity") and set `dp[0] = 0`, since the frog starts there and needs no energy.

3. **Fill order:** Loop `i` from `1` to `n - 1`. When we compute `dp[i]`, every `dp[i-j]` is already final because `i - j < i`.

4. **Transition:** For each jump length `j` from `1` to `k`, if `i - j >= 0` (the jump stays inside the array), update:

   `dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[i-j] + |heights[i] - heights[i-j]|)`

5. **Answer:** Return `dp[n-1]`.

**Edge cases handled:**

- `n == 1`: the loop never runs, and `dp[0] = 0` is returned.
- `k > i`: the check `i - j >= 0` prevents out-of-bounds access, so only valid earlier stairs are considered.
- `k >= n`: same as above, the frog simply considers every earlier stair.

**Why it is correct:** every path to index `i` must arrive from one of the `k` stairs just before it. We try all of them, and each `dp[i-j]` is already the true optimum for that stair. So the minimum over all choices is the true optimum for `i`.

## Solution

```java[]
class Solution {
    public int frogJump(int[] heights, int k) {
        int n=heights.length;
        int[] dp = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(dp,(int)1e9);
        dp[0]=0;
        for(int i=1;i<n;i++){
            for(int j=1;j<=k;j++){
            if(i-j>=0){
                dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i],Math.abs(dp[i-j] + Math.abs(heights[i]-heights[i-j])));
            }  
        }
        }
        return dp[n-1];
    }
}
```

## Complexity

**Time Complexity:**
O(n · k). The outer loop runs `n - 1` times, and for each index the inner loop tries up to `k` previous stairs. Each transition is O(1).

**Space Complexity:**
O(n). The `dp` array uses `O(n)` extra memory, and there is no recursion stack.8

## Intuition
This is the classic **Frog Jump with K distances** problem, and it has **optimal substructure**. The minimum energy needed to reach stair `i` depends only on the minimum energy needed to reach one of the previous `k` stairs (`i-1, i-2, ..., i-k`), plus the cost of the final jump from that stair to `i`.

If `f(i)` is the minimum energy to reach index `i`:

`f(i) = min over j in [1..k] of ( f(i-j) + |heights[i] - heights[i-j]| )`

Plain recursion recomputes the same subproblems (`f(i-1)`, `f(i-2)`, ...) many times, which blows up exponentially. **Memoization** (top-down DP) stores each `f(i)` once, so every index is solved only once.

## Approach
1. **Define the state:** `dp[ind]` stores the minimum energy required to reach index `ind` starting from index `0`.
2. **Base case:** If `ind == 0`, the frog is already at the start, so the cost is `0`.
3. **Memo check:** `dp` is initialised to `1e9` (acting as "not computed yet"). If `dp[ind]` is anything other than `1e9`, return it immediately.
4. **Transition:** For every jump length `i` from `1` to `k`, if `ind - i >= 0` (the jump stays inside the array), compute the cost of coming from `ind - i`:
   `rec(ind - i) + |heights[ind] - heights[ind - i]|`
   and keep the minimum in `dp[ind]`.
5. **Answer:** Call `rec` on the last index `n - 1`.

**Edge cases handled:**
- `n == 1`: the recursion starts at index `0` and returns `0` directly.
- `k > ind`: the check `ind - i >= 0` prevents out-of-bounds jumps, so only valid previous stairs are considered.
- `k >= n`: same as above, the frog simply considers every earlier stair.

**Why it is correct:** every path to `ind` must arrive from one of the `k` stairs before it. Since we try all of them and each subproblem is solved optimally (recursively), the minimum over all choices is the true optimum.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    private int rec(int[] heights, int k, int ind, int n, int[] dp){
        if(ind==0)return 0;
        if(dp[ind]!=(int)1e9) return dp[ind];
        for(int i=1;i<=k;i++){
            if(ind-i>=0){
                dp[ind] =Math.min(dp[ind],rec(heights,k,ind-i,n,dp) + Math.abs(heights[ind]-heights[ind-i]));
            }

        }
        return dp[ind];
    }
    public int frogJump(int[] heights, int k) {
        int n=heights.length;
        int[] dp = new int[n+1];
        Arrays.fill(dp,(int)1e9);
        dp[0]=0;
        return rec(heights,k,n-1,n,dp);
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(n · k). There are `n` distinct states, and each state is computed once (thanks to memoization). Computing a state loops over up to `k` previous stairs.

**Space Complexity:**
O(n). The `dp` array uses `O(n)` extra memory, and the recursion stack can go as deep as `n` in the worst case (e.g., when `k = 1`), which is also `O(n)`.8
